alse,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"C072\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":0.5,\"reason\":\"The answer says UIT-structured ETFs 'require orders,' which correctly places UITs outside the rule. It never states that Rule 6c-11 covers registered open-end ETFs.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":0,\"reason\":\"The answer says leveraged/inverse ETFs are 'excluded from the rule, so they continue under individual exemptive orders.' That is the opposite of the required minimum, which is that they may rely on 6c-11 subject to Rule 18f-4.\"},{\"point\":3,\"credit\":0.5,\"reason\":\"The answer says non-transparent active ETFs and share-class ETFs require orders, and it notes that 6c-11 mandates daily holdings disclosure. It omits master-feeder structures, so the bundle is incomplete.\"}],\"central_error\":\"\\\"Leveraged and inverse ETFs: excluded from the rule, so they continue under individual exemptive orders.\\\" This matches the central error 'Leveraged/inverse categorically excluded.' The closing line 'Leveraged/inverse funds must satisfy 18f-4 alongside their exemptive orders' repeats the same error.\",\"local_error\":\"\\\"Rule 6c-11 (effective 2024)\\\" is a material ancillary falsehood. The rule was adopted in 2019 and became effective in December 2019, with compliance required in 2020.\",\"grade\":\"wrong\",\"rationale\":\"The answer explicitly states that leveraged/inverse ETFs are categorically excluded from Rule 6c-11 and need individual exemptive orders. This is a listed central error and forces a grade of wrong. Required coverage totals 1.0 of 3, which is also below half. The effective-date claim is a separate local falsehood.\"}","ttft_ms":2602,"type":"result","duration_ms":6911,"uuid":"f07f8dd0-0c2f-4821-9504-c296775b76f7","ttft_stream_ms":1048,"time_to_request_ms":70,"first_content_frame_ms":1048,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
